modern societies are pretty much in  agreement on this score  having children is one of the most  meaningful and delightful moves  that anyone can make couples who don't  for whatever reason  have children tend to be automatically  almost universally  pitied and are assumed to have been  denied the chance to have offspring  by biology that one might freely choose  not to have children and yet be  reasonably content with one's choice  that remains one of the most disturbing  and  unfathomed of all modern positions  the basic dynamics of whether or not to  have children follow  the very same pattern that we see across  a range  of other so-called great choices in  emotional life  whether or not to get married whether or  not to stay  faithful whether to follow the path of  reason  or the cause of the heart we observe  a very strong desire to try to identify  the right choice  accompanied by a frighteningly utopian  belief that once this choice has been  located  we're going to be able to flourish and  find complete  peace and happiness but the reality  is of course very different much darker  but also more interesting the large  dilemmas of emotional life  have no answer in the sense of a  response that doesn't somewhere along  the line  entail a great loss and an element of  extraordinary sacrifice  whatever we choose will in a sense be a  bit  wrong and leave us regretting some  features of the choices  we didn't make there is no such thing as  a cost-free choice  a line of argument which continues oddly  to create surprise  in contemporary life making a good  choice  simply involves focusing on what variety  of suffering  we're best suited to rather than aiming  with some sort of utopian zeal  to try to avoid grief and regret  altogether  consider for example the varieties of  suffering that are on offer  on both sides of the faithful unfaithful  ledger both options will at moments be  very miserable so when weighing up how  to lead our lives  we should work on knowing as much as  possible about our specific taste  in misery let's look at a table monogamy  the misery well a sense of confinement  a correct impression that life is  elsewhere irritability  narrow horizons sexual abandonment  what about on the other side multiple  partners  what will be the misery there there'll  be chaos angry exes  loneliness long term damaged children  guilt  the very same kind of trade-offs exist  over the question  of children no honest experience of  parenting  is complete without an intermittent very  strong impression  that in some ways children are both the  meaning of one's life  and the cause of the ruin of one's life  on the one hand children the misery  disappointment with oneself as a parent  disappointment with how they turn out  guilt exhaustion lost opportunity  a sense of perpetuating human suffering  the house  sticky everywhere what about with no  children  what's the misery there society's  constant message  that one's missed out loneliness and  boredom  a lack of constant distraction and calls  on one's time  and a sentimental longing for comfort of  children  by the time one reaches the nursing home  the insight that all choices are in a  sense a bit  hellish was best expressed by the early  19th century danish  existential philosopher soren kikegaard  who summed up our options in a playful  but bleakly realistic and exasperated  outburst in his masterpiece either or  marry and you will regret it don't marry  you will also regret it  marry or don't marry you will regret it  either way  laugh at the world's foolishness you'll  regret it weep over it  you'll regret that too laugh at the  world's foolishness or weep over it  you'll regret both  believe a woman you'll regret it believe  her not  you'll also regret it hang yourself  you'll regret it don't hang yourself  you'll regret that too hang yourself or  don't hang yourself you'll regret it  either way  whether you hang yourself or don't hang  yourself you'll regret both  this gentleman is the essence of all  philosophy  we deserve pity as does everyone else  we're going to make disastrous decisions  we'll form mistaken relationships  we'll embark on misguided careers we'll  invest our savings foolishly we'll spend  years on friendships with unreliable  idiots  and we'll get it mostly wrong around  children  but we can be consoled by a bitter truth  there  aren't painless options because the  conditions of existence are  intrinsically  rather than accidentally frustrating we  can't get through the tunnel of life  without a mauling  for those of us contemplating whether or  not to have children  the message is dark but consoling in its  bleakness  you will be at points very unhappy  whatever you choose  with either option you will feel that  you've ruined  your life and you'll be correct we don't  need to add to our misery  by insisting that there would have been  another better  cost-free way there is curiously  relief to be found in the knowledge of  the inevitability of suffering  it is in the end never darkness that  dooms us  but the wrong sort of hope in that most  cruel  of fantasies the right choice  the joys and sorrows of parenting  promises us a gentle way of staying calm  around one of the most  arduous yet deeply fulfilling jobs in  the world  you
 

